# Deletion and deprecation

Deleting something design-data already shipped breaks consumers. Deleting
something made in this session does not. The full proposal is
`docs/rfcs/deletion-and-deprecation.md` (LTAX-024).

## The rule

| Item | What Delete does |
| --- | --- |
| Not shipped (made in this session) | Deletes. Toast "{Item} deleted" with Undo |
| Shipped | Opens "Delete {kind}" with Rename instead, Deprecate and replace, or Deprecate. Never deletes |
| Shipped and already deprecated | Explains that only Remove all deprecations can remove it |

"Shipped" means `isShipped(collection, id)` (`src/features/changelog/shipped.ts`):
the ID is in the shipped change snapshot. Do not invent another test.

## Every new delete

1. Classify the notification kind in `LIFECYCLE_KINDS`
   (`src/features/notifications/lifecycleKinds.ts`):
   - `lifecycle`: reaches design-data output and follows this rule.
   - `{ planned: reason }`: reaches output but is not migrated yet. Add a
     DEBT-046 note if you add one.
   - `{ exempt: reason }`: Studio-only data or a small part of an item.
   New kinds that reach output must be `lifecycle`.
2. For `lifecycle` kinds, pass a `lifecycle` descriptor to `notify.remove`.
   The Registry term delete (`TaxonomyView.remove`) is the reference:

   ```tsx
   notify.remove({
     kind: "term",
     run: (store) => /* delete */,
     undo: (receipt) => /* restore */,
     lifecycle: {
       shipped: isShipped("terms", term.id),
       deprecated: isDeprecated(term),
       rename: {
         current: term.value,
         run: (text) => change((doc) => renameTaxonomyTerm(doc, term.id, text)),
       },
       candidates: termReplacementCandidates(taxonomy, term.id),
       deprecate: (replacedBy) =>
         change((doc) => deprecateTaxonomyTerm(doc, term.id, replacedBy)),
       undoDeprecate: (receipt) => commit(receipt.before, receipt.after),
     },
   });
   ```

   Data Studio always authors (for example aliases) passes
   `lifecycle: { shipped: false }` with a comment saying why.
3. Do not add your own delete dialog. `LifecycleDialog` is shown by
   `NotificationProvider`.

## Paths in the dialog

- **Rename instead**: only for simple items whose value can change in place
  (a Registry term, or a color scale whose name is a Registry term). It keeps
  every ID, so mappings survive. Omit `rename` for complex items such as
  categories, or scales without a registered name.
- **Deprecate and replace**: `candidates` are active items of the same kind.
  Tokens must match token type. Scales must match step for step (see
  `colorScaleReplacementCandidates`). Omit candidates when none can be 1:1.
- **Deprecate**: always offered when `deprecate` is set.

## Lifecycle data

- Use `ItemLifecycle` and helpers from `src/features/edit-session/lifecycle.ts`
  (`deprecatedLifecycle`, `withLifecycle`, `isDeprecated`, `lifecycleIssue`).
  Do not add another lifecycle shape. Tokens keep their authoring lifecycle.
- `lifecycle` is optional and present only when deprecated. Remove it to
  reactivate an item.
- Leave `deprecatedIn` empty; release tooling assigns it (DEBT-035).
- Validate `replacedBy` in the model's validator: it must exist and be the
  same kind. Only offer active items when choosing.
- Show `DeprecatedBadge` (`src/components/DeprecatedBadge.tsx`) wherever the
  item is listed.
- Changes parity: lifecycle fields must show and reset in Changes. The
  comparator (`src/features/changelog/registry.ts`) shows any `lifecycle` key
  as one field, such as "Deprecated, replaced by Red", and resets it as a
  whole, because a partial lifecycle is invalid. Add a `reviewField` case in
  `tests/deprecation-removal.ts`.

## Remove all deprecations

`src/features/changelog/removeDeprecations.ts` lists, previews and removes
deprecated items. Token decisions come from the pure planner
`src/features/changelog/deprecationRemoval.ts` (`planTokenRemoval`):

- Uses of a removed item move to its final active replacement
  (`finalTokenReplacement`, `finalTermReplacement`).
- A removed token with no replacement is hard-coded per mode where a component
  or value slot uses it.
- Anything that cannot be handled safely is kept and reported in `kept` with a
  reason. Never delete something that is still in use without one of these.
- Removed shipped tokens are recorded as `removed: true` in token edits; do not
  edit source data.

The result is `{ removed, kept, repointed, hardCoded, undo }`. Show
`previewSummary` before confirming and offer `undo` (restored with
`restoreAuthoringSnapshotAtom`) in the toast. When you make a kind
`lifecycle`, add it to `deprecatedItems`, the planner and the apply atom, and
add planner tests.

Studio is not connected to GitHub yet. "Shipped" means the bundled defaults,
and removals stay in the browser. Do not add code that assumes a published
release or a repository write. When your change depends on one of these, add
a row to "Current limits and future GitHub wiring" in
`docs/rfcs/deletion-and-deprecation.md` and update DEBT-047.

## Enforcement

- `tests/notification-usage.ts` fails when a `notify.remove` call for a
  `lifecycle` kind has no `lifecycle:` descriptor.
- `tests/notifications.ts` covers the shipped, unshipped and deprecated flows.
- `tests/deprecation-removal.ts` covers lifecycle Changes parity, rename,
  deprecation, the removal planner, Remove all deprecations and Undo.
